2026-01-11 19:33:26,172 - INFO - Tool call: write_file({"file_path":"/tmp/work/analysis.md","content":"# Exploit attempt notes (exec-shell-cfi)\n\n## Summary\n\nThe Atomics+Resizable-ArrayBuffer bug provides a post-callback stale pointer used by the atomic primitive. This gives a powerful UAF/OOB primitive that can be used to corrupt glibc heap metadata (e.g., safe-linked tcache `fd`).\n\nI built:\n\n1. **libc leak** via shrinking a large RAB to create an unsorted-bin remainder chunk and reading its `fd` pointer with `Atomics.compareExchange`.\n2. **tcache poisoning** via shrinking a 0x420-byte RAB to create a 0x410 tcache remainder chunk and corrupting its `fd` using `Atomics.xor`.\n3. A plan to overwrite `__exit_funcs` (Full RELRO-friendly) to call `system(\"/bin/sh\")` at `std.exit()`.\n\n## Critical blocker discovered\n\nglibc stores exit-handler function pointers **mangled** using the per-thread pointer guard (`PTR_MANGLE` / `PTR_DEMANGLE` using `%fs:0x30`).\n\nA runtime memory dump of `__exit_funcs` shows the handler function pointer is not a canonical pointer and is clearly mangled.\n\nTherefore, writing a raw `system` address into the exit handler does **not** work; it must be mangled with the thread pointer guard.\n\nTo complete the exploit, we must:\n\n- Leak the pointer guard (`%fs:0x30`) or derive it from a known unmangled pointer and an existing mangled pointer.\n- Compute `mangled_system = rol(system ^ guard, 0x11)` and write it into the exit entry.\n\nLeaking the pointer guard (or deriving it) was not completed.\n\n## Current PoC status\n\n`/tmp/work/poc.js` demonstrates:\n\n- The libc leak\n- the tcache poisoning and ability to redirect allocations into libc `.bss`\n\n…but it does not yet spawn a shell because the exit-handler function pointer requires pointer mangling.\n"})
