2026-01-11 19:39:04,669 - INFO - Tool call: write_file({"file_path":"/tmp/work/analysis.md","content":"# Heap exploit notes: Atomics + Resizable ArrayBuffer (QuickJS) => attempted exec-shell-cfi\n\n## What works\n\n### 1) libc base leak (ASLR bypass)\n\nUsing `Atomics.compareExchange(BigInt64Array, idx, evil, 1n)`:\n\n- `js_atomics_get_ptr()` captures `ptr = ab->data + idx*8`.\n- `evil.valueOf()` shrinks the RAB (`ab.resize(8)`), splitting the original malloc chunk.\n- For a large original buffer (`0x1000` bytes), the remainder chunk goes to the **unsorted bin**.\n- The atomic op reads the unsorted chunk's `fd` pointer (a libc pointer).\n\nFrom that we compute:\n\n- `libc_base = unsorted_fd - 0x1e7b20` (glibc 2.41 main_arena unsorted fd offset).\n\n### 2) Controlled corruption of tcache metadata\n\nFor an original RAB of length `0x420`, shrinking to 8 produces a remainder chunk of size `0x410` which goes to **tcache**.\n\nThe stale-pointer bug lets us write to the freed remainder chunk's `fd` field using `Atomics.xor`/`exchange`.\n\nThis is enough to redirect subsequent `malloc(0x400)` to a chosen address (tcache poisoning).\n\n## Critical blocker for the exit-handler technique\n\nI attempted to overwrite glibc `__exit_funcs` (inside libc `.bss`) so that `std.exit()` triggers:\n\n- `system(\"/bin/sh\")`.\n\nHowever, glibc stores exit-handler function pointers **mangled** with the per-thread pointer guard:\n\n- `PTR_MANGLE(p) = rol(p ^ guard, 0x11)`\n- `PTR_DEMANGLE(p) = ror(p, 0x11) ^ guard`\n- `guard` is stored in TLS at `%fs:0x30`.\n\nA memory dump of `__exit_funcs` shows the stored function pointer is not a canonical pointer (mangled), so writing a raw `system` address will not be called correctly.\n\n### What is still needed\n\nTo complete the exit-handler exploit path:\n\n1. Leak or derive the TLS pointer guard (`%fs:0x30`).\n2. Compute `mangled_system = rol(system_addr ^ guard, 0x11)`.\n3. Overwrite the `__exit_funcs` entry with:\n   - flavor = 4 (ef_cxa)\n   - fn = mangled_system\n   - arg = \"/bin/sh\"\n   - dso = 0\n\n### Promising direction (not finished)\n\nIf we can also compute the PIE base of `qjs`, we can derive the pointer guard from the already-registered atexit handler:\n\n- Read mangled `term_exit` from `__exit_funcs`.\n- Compute `term_exit` address as `qjs_base + term_exit_off`.\n- Then: `guard = ror(mangled_term_exit, 0x11) ^ term_exit`.\n\nI found `term_exit` offset in the qjs binary (`nm qjs | grep term_exit` => `0x103990`).\n\nA constant offset between a heap page (leaked as `keyA<<12`) and the PIE base can be measured offline (example gdb run produced `DELTA=0x33922b0`), enabling an estimate of `qjs_base` from a heap page leak.\n\nBut I did not finish a robust, in-JS method to read the mangled `term_exit` value without clobbering it.\n\n## Current PoC\n\n`/tmp/work/poc.js` contains:\n\n- the libc leak\n- tcache poisoning logic\n\n…but it does **not** yet spawn a shell because pointer mangling is not handled.\n"})
